# Group Policy Management

## Lab Overview
This lab focuses on applyingn GPOs to OUs. Central management of users and computer settings and testing of the configurations.

## Objectives
- Explore the different capabilities of Group Policy Management
- Create and Apply GPOs to OUs
- Verify the GPOs have been applied to domain devices

## Key Concepts
| GPO(Group Policy Object) | Set of configuration rules applied to domain-joined users and computers |
| GPM(Group Policy Management )| Microsoft admin tool used to create, edit, link and troubleshoot GPOs |
| SYSVOL | folder shared by all DCs in a Domain. Ensures policies/scripts are replicated to all domain-joined accounts |

## Environment
Platform: TryHackMe  
Operating System: Windows Server 2019
Software: Group Policy Management Console

## Lab Walkthrough

### Change minimum password length for domain-joined computers
- Select 'Default Domain Policy' > Select 'Edit' > Select Computer Configurations > Security Settings > Account Policies > Password Policies > Change Required Policy Value
- This is a high-priority step when configuring any domain.

### Restrict Access to Control Panel
- Create new GPO(called "restrict control panel access") > Select 'Edit' > Click  User Configuration > Click Policies > Click Adniminstrative Templates > Contro Pannel > Enable 'Prohibit Access to Control Panel and PC settings' > Drag the GPO to each OU that shouldn't have access to the Control Panel
- This is completed to mitigate issues due to misconfigurations or actions of malicious users

### Force Computer to sync the GPO(immediately)
- Run Powershell as Admin > Type: gpupdate /force
- When a GPO has been made, it may take up to (2) hours to sync. It may be necessary for critical configuration changes to be applied immediately


